            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Stephen E. Eberhardt, stephen E. Eberhardt questioned why Congress would want district courts to continue imposing sentences that were universally viewed as unfair and racially discriminatory."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Michael R. Dreeben",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2af923395ca5-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 15,
              "start": 15
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Michael R. Dreeben, michael R. Dreeben stated that the Fair Sentencing Act manifests the requisite implication that Congress intended its new mandatory\u2011minimum thresholds to apply in all sentencings after the date of the Act."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 23,
              "start": 23
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Mr. Dreeben argued that there will probably be thousands of crack defendants who will be sentenced under the old mandatory minimums that Congress repealed because they were perceived as racially disparate and unfair."
      }
    ],
